WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:03.169
-Our first guest tonight is a
Grammy-nominated comedian

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:04.304
and actor you know from
shows like

00:00:04.304 --> 00:00:06.306
"Breaking Bad"
and "The Mandalorian"

00:00:06.306 --> 00:00:09.008
and movies like "The Heat" and
"The King of Staten Island."

00:00:09.008 --> 00:00:10.810
The vinyl release of

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:12.912
"Bill Burr
Live From Madison Square Garden"

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:15.782
is available at amoeba.com
September 23rd.

00:00:15.782 --> 00:00:17.950
Please welcome back to
the show Bill Burr.

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:19.919
Bill, how are you, my friend?

00:00:19.919 --> 00:00:21.120
-What's going on?

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.489
I loved that dramatic pause
after "Grammy."

00:00:23.489 --> 00:00:25.525
I got excited like,
"Wait, did I win?"

00:00:25.525 --> 00:00:27.893
"This guy is a
Grammy...nominated..."

00:00:27.893 --> 00:00:29.128
Damn it!

00:00:29.128 --> 00:00:31.563
-I wanted you to go through
that whole moment,

00:00:31.563 --> 00:00:33.499
that whole
emotional journey again.

00:00:33.499 --> 00:00:35.034
-Oh, it was great. I was there.

00:00:35.034 --> 00:00:36.902
They had the thing
sitting in front of me like

00:00:36.902 --> 00:00:39.706
they were going to hand it off
to me like a running back.

00:00:39.706 --> 00:00:41.274
"And the winner is..."
someone else.

00:00:41.274 --> 00:00:44.310
And then the guy just turns
away, and five seconds later,

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.712
you're standing in the
parking lot with your suit on,

00:00:46.712 --> 00:00:48.914
wondering what the hell
just happened.

00:00:48.914 --> 00:00:51.984
-But this is very --
This is really cool.

00:00:51.984 --> 00:00:54.220
This is kind of a throw-back
to what comedy albums

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.422
used to look like
way back in the '70s,

00:00:56.422 --> 00:00:57.857
sort of a big double album.

00:00:57.857 --> 00:01:00.527
And it's sort of also
a historical document

00:01:00.527 --> 00:01:02.595
for when you played
Madison Square Garden.

00:01:02.595 --> 00:01:07.133
-Yeah, I wanted to --
When I play these places

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:09.602
that I never thought
I would play --

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:12.672
I made one at Carnegie Hall --

00:01:12.672 --> 00:01:14.373
I'm doing it for me and
then just, like,

00:01:14.373 --> 00:01:15.809
big-time comedy nerds.

00:01:15.809 --> 00:01:18.645
That's why I put it on vinyl,
because I did "Paper Tiger"

00:01:18.645 --> 00:01:20.747
like four months after this.

00:01:20.747 --> 00:01:22.915
So this is sort of where
my act was,

00:01:22.915 --> 00:01:27.453
and it's, you know, a half-hour
longer, I think, than --

00:01:27.453 --> 00:01:28.554
There's a whole side of it

00:01:28.554 --> 00:01:30.623
that's not even on
"Paper Tiger,"

00:01:30.623 --> 00:01:32.425
and it was this whole chunk
that I wanted

00:01:32.425 --> 00:01:34.960
to have in "Paper Tiger"
where I was making fun of

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:38.031
the whole "support the troops,
firemen are heroes,"

00:01:38.031 --> 00:01:39.032
all of that stuff.

00:01:39.032 --> 00:01:40.466
So all of that is on there.

00:01:40.466 --> 00:01:44.303
It's just -- You know,
it's sort of a weird --

00:01:44.303 --> 00:01:45.604
What the hell happened?

00:01:45.604 --> 00:01:48.741
Oh, when I was in London,
there was some terrorist attack,

00:01:48.741 --> 00:01:50.075
so that just put
a weird sort of --

00:01:50.075 --> 00:01:51.210
And, for some reason,

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:53.846
they didn't tell me
that that happened.

00:01:53.846 --> 00:01:55.281
"We don't need it to be
in his head,"

00:01:55.281 --> 00:01:59.118
so the information I needed to
make it work I didn't have.

00:01:59.118 --> 00:02:01.921
So, we took it out, but it
ended up being a great thing

00:02:01.921 --> 00:02:04.723
for the special because,
you know,

00:02:04.723 --> 00:02:06.425
I think people
just sitting down --

00:02:06.425 --> 00:02:07.960
Like, on Netflix,
there's so much stuff to watch,

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:09.328
you kind of want to
get in and get out.

00:02:09.328 --> 00:02:14.200
So -- But this is the long, you
know, if you're at home,

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:15.601
you're on drugs, you know,

00:02:15.601 --> 00:02:17.036
you've got to cut up whatever
you're cutting up,

00:02:17.036 --> 00:02:19.205
I've got the vinyl album
there for you.

00:02:19.205 --> 00:02:20.840
Old school --
put on your headphones.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.141
-I love it.

00:02:22.141 --> 00:02:24.611
You -- I'm wondering,
after you play one of these

00:02:24.611 --> 00:02:26.879
historic spaces like
Madison Square Garden,

00:02:26.879 --> 00:02:29.582
does it make it harder
to go to smaller venues

00:02:29.582 --> 00:02:33.553
or is it exactly exciting
as that was before, too?

00:02:33.553 --> 00:02:38.591
-No, I like doing that
as, like, a --

00:02:38.591 --> 00:02:40.960
I like going back into
the trenches.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:42.428
Like, I have never been
a person like,

00:02:42.428 --> 00:02:46.198
after you do the big thing,
you then have to go to the club

00:02:46.198 --> 00:02:48.568
in like the three S.U.V.s,
and then people have to

00:02:48.568 --> 00:02:50.069
make sure it's okay
for you to be --

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:52.238
You just make this big thing.

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:53.773
You just make this big thing
so everybody is looking,

00:02:53.773 --> 00:02:54.974
and they say, "See?

00:02:54.974 --> 00:02:56.775
This is what happens.
I can't go anywhere."

00:02:56.775 --> 00:02:58.544
It's like, "Well,
if you would just walk in,

00:02:58.544 --> 00:03:00.045
no one would care."

00:03:00.045 --> 00:03:04.116
So, I am -- After I listen to
my own stupid voice

00:03:04.116 --> 00:03:06.419
for an hour or hour and a half,
I go the exact opposite.

00:03:06.419 --> 00:03:07.787
I like a dive bar.

00:03:07.787 --> 00:03:10.823
Back when I used to booze,
I would go to, like, a dive bar.

00:03:10.823 --> 00:03:12.658
I remember doing The Beacon

00:03:12.658 --> 00:03:14.260
and I didn't even really have
an after party.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.495
We just found
the nearest dive bar,

00:03:16.495 --> 00:03:18.697
and I remember some woman
there was going like,

00:03:18.697 --> 00:03:20.533
"This isn't what, like,
an after party should be.

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:22.101
Like, you just played
that place.

00:03:22.101 --> 00:03:25.171
This should be,
like, nicer and everything."

00:03:25.171 --> 00:03:26.706
It's like, "Why?
What's wrong with this place?"

00:03:26.706 --> 00:03:30.043
I hate those places --
when I was drinking --

00:03:30.043 --> 00:03:33.178
that it took forever
to get a drink.

00:03:33.178 --> 00:03:35.081
And if you're a guy,
they just didn't give a [bleep]

00:03:35.081 --> 00:03:37.616
I used to have to
send my wife up.

00:03:37.616 --> 00:03:39.218
I'm like, "You know,
you're good-looking.

00:03:39.218 --> 00:03:40.953
Go up. They'll talk to you.

00:03:40.953 --> 00:03:42.655
Maybe he thinks he's going to
bang you or something,

00:03:42.655 --> 00:03:44.557
and maybe I can get a beer.

00:03:44.557 --> 00:03:48.827
So, I like the --
I like those bars back --

00:03:48.827 --> 00:03:50.596
Oh, man, I miss drinking.

00:03:50.596 --> 00:03:52.098
[ Laughter ]

00:03:52.098 --> 00:03:53.633
I used to love day drinking.

00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:55.534
-Should we change the
subject before

00:03:55.534 --> 00:03:56.535
you get too far down
this road of missing it?

00:03:56.535 --> 00:03:57.604
-No, no.

00:03:57.604 --> 00:03:59.204
I just remember
how hard it was to quit.

00:03:59.204 --> 00:04:00.339
I'm not going to go back.

00:04:00.339 --> 00:04:02.909
But I'll tell you,
every once in a while,

00:04:02.909 --> 00:04:04.777
I'll just see a bar
that I would have gone into.

00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:05.978
-Yeah.

00:04:05.978 --> 00:04:08.214
-And I used to love
going in during the day

00:04:08.214 --> 00:04:10.283
with the pro drinkers, you know?

00:04:10.283 --> 00:04:11.984
And you'd come
walking in, and they'd --

00:04:11.984 --> 00:04:13.986
You know, they'd just give
you that glance.

00:04:13.986 --> 00:04:16.722
And then, when you
weren't loud and you sat down

00:04:16.722 --> 00:04:20.193
and you ordered a man's drink --
you know what I mean? --

00:04:20.193 --> 00:04:23.062
and then you just -- then they
knew that you had your drink

00:04:23.062 --> 00:04:24.997
and you were just going to
stare down at the bar

00:04:24.997 --> 00:04:28.634
and think about your regrets
and not disturb anybody.

00:04:28.634 --> 00:04:32.171
It was like -- Like, day
drinking by yourself, you know,

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:33.706
as much as they say,
"Don't do it,"

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:35.775
is one of the big thrills
I had as an adult.

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:38.644
-Yeah, I love that,
like, soft wood

00:04:38.644 --> 00:04:42.181
where people could carve their
misery in for the next person,

00:04:42.181 --> 00:04:44.583
you know, just, like,
half a message of sadness

00:04:44.583 --> 00:04:45.985
that then you,
when you had your drink,

00:04:45.985 --> 00:04:48.220
could look down and
realize you're not alone.

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:51.791
-Yeah, the creepy, like, playful
flirting the older drunks

00:04:51.791 --> 00:04:53.892
were doing with
the younger waitress,

00:04:53.892 --> 00:04:56.862
and watching her knowing
how to play it perfectly

00:04:56.862 --> 00:04:58.163
so she'd still get tips.

00:04:58.163 --> 00:05:00.566
I mean, yeah,
it was a lot of fun.

00:05:00.566 --> 00:05:05.103
-You're doing a signing of this
at Amoeba in L.A.

00:05:05.103 --> 00:05:08.074
Will this be a situation where
you will ask your fans

00:05:08.074 --> 00:05:09.441
to wear a mask when they come in

00:05:09.441 --> 00:05:11.477
and will you refuse
the unmasked?

00:05:11.477 --> 00:05:13.345
-I will definitely be
wearing a mask,

00:05:13.345 --> 00:05:16.882
but I'm at the point now,
I don't care anymore.

00:05:16.882 --> 00:05:18.217
-Mm-hmm.
-I don't care.

00:05:18.217 --> 00:05:19.919
I don't care if you think
the world's flat.

00:05:19.919 --> 00:05:21.654
I don't care what you think.

00:05:21.654 --> 00:05:23.656
Just do whatever the hell
you're going to do.

00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:26.325
I hope the virus gets deadlier

00:05:26.325 --> 00:05:30.062
and I want it to just
wipe out way more people.

00:05:30.062 --> 00:05:31.430
-Yeah.
-That's what I'm going for.

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:32.731
Because, you know, we tried --

00:05:32.731 --> 00:05:35.200
I think we all tried to pull
in the same direction.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:37.003
It's not going to happen.
-Right.

00:05:37.003 --> 00:05:38.604
-And we're all sort of broken up

00:05:38.604 --> 00:05:40.139
into these pods on the Internet

00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:42.174
where people just want to hear
what they want to hear.

00:05:42.174 --> 00:05:43.809
So, I mean, go ahead.

00:05:43.809 --> 00:05:45.978
You know, go take
alligator plaque medicine,

00:05:45.978 --> 00:05:47.212
whatever the hell you're into.

00:05:47.212 --> 00:05:49.448
I don't -- Maybe it works.
I don't know.

00:05:49.448 --> 00:05:52.618
I don't care. I'm just...

00:05:52.618 --> 00:05:55.053
-You know, you have always had
the position that we are --

00:05:55.053 --> 00:05:57.990
we have a population issue
in this world.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:05:59.592
Like, you believe we have
too many people.

00:05:59.592 --> 00:06:01.026
So I guess every time
you see one of this,

00:06:01.026 --> 00:06:02.195
this is both --
this is an opportunity --

00:06:02.195 --> 00:06:05.098
-People are blaming
red ties and blue ties

00:06:05.098 --> 00:06:06.565
for this whole thing,
and that's not --

00:06:06.565 --> 00:06:10.335
You should blame God
and modern medicine,

00:06:10.335 --> 00:06:12.038
because God made people flawed,

00:06:12.038 --> 00:06:15.007
and modern medicine kept
them all alive.

00:06:15.007 --> 00:06:16.275
Defeated Mother Nature.

00:06:16.275 --> 00:06:17.476
That's why there's

00:06:17.476 --> 00:06:19.946
8 billion mouth-breathing
morons on the planet,

00:06:19.946 --> 00:06:21.079
myself included.

00:06:21.079 --> 00:06:24.049
I should have died of an
appendicitis in 1980

00:06:24.049 --> 00:06:27.319
under a stack of wool blankets
and people rubbing garlic

00:06:27.319 --> 00:06:29.388
on my forehead or whatever the
hell they used to do.

00:06:29.388 --> 00:06:31.591
But, instead,
they kept me alive,

00:06:31.591 --> 00:06:33.158
so now you have to
listen to my ignorance.

00:06:33.158 --> 00:06:34.159
It's a plague.

00:06:34.159 --> 00:06:37.529
There's a plague of people
like me.

00:06:37.529 --> 00:06:39.131
So, I think
it's great that people

00:06:39.131 --> 00:06:42.034
are going to go old school and
not listen to doctors

00:06:42.034 --> 00:06:45.671
and, you know,
go back to doing dances

00:06:45.671 --> 00:06:50.409
or whatever the hell
they're going to do.

00:06:50.409 --> 00:06:53.679
-We need more healthcare dances.

00:06:53.679 --> 00:06:55.481
We've lost that.
-Yeah. Let's go.

00:06:55.481 --> 00:06:57.883
Go back -- Whatever the hell
you want to do.

00:06:57.883 --> 00:07:02.454
I don't -- I cared right up
until when the vaccine came out,

00:07:02.454 --> 00:07:03.588
and people are still like,
"I'm not taking it."

00:07:03.588 --> 00:07:04.790
Then I was just like,

00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:06.158
"Alright, well,
then I don't care anymore."

00:07:06.158 --> 00:07:07.860
-Yeah.
-I'm not rooting for them.

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:09.128
-I agree with you 100%.

00:07:09.128 --> 00:07:10.462
I was so excited to get it,

00:07:10.462 --> 00:07:12.898
and I couldn't believe that was
not shared by everybody.

00:07:12.898 --> 00:07:14.533
You go out --
-I get it.

00:07:14.533 --> 00:07:15.835
I mean, listen, you know,

00:07:15.835 --> 00:07:17.503
Big Pharmaceutical hasn't
exactly --

00:07:17.503 --> 00:07:19.705
I mean, they also created,
like, you know,

00:07:19.705 --> 00:07:22.308
the opioid epidemic,
and people have lost their kids

00:07:22.308 --> 00:07:24.444
and they're not
held accountable at all.

00:07:24.444 --> 00:07:25.944
Are they, Seth Meyers?
-They are not.

00:07:25.944 --> 00:07:28.781
-They are not, but if you tell
the wrong joke in a strip mall,

00:07:28.781 --> 00:07:31.216
oh, my God,
you lose your career.

00:07:31.216 --> 00:07:32.952
It's so stupid.

00:07:32.952 --> 00:07:34.954
The stuff that people
are paying attention to --

00:07:34.954 --> 00:07:37.322
I had a buddy of mine
last year freaked out

00:07:37.322 --> 00:07:42.061
because they made
Mr. Potato Head nonbinary.

00:07:42.061 --> 00:07:44.396
He's like, "You can't say
Mr. Potato Head anymore."

00:07:44.396 --> 00:07:46.565
It's like, "You can.
You just did."

00:07:46.565 --> 00:07:49.769
And they don't care about people
that want to transition.

00:07:49.769 --> 00:07:52.471
This is a marketing ploy,
alright?

00:07:52.471 --> 00:07:55.307
There's 24 hours of free porn.

00:07:55.307 --> 00:07:57.075
You can ride around on a Segway

00:07:57.075 --> 00:07:58.710
with the
virtual-reality glasses.

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:00.912
These guys are selling
a plastic potato

00:08:00.912 --> 00:08:03.782
you stick things into.

00:08:03.782 --> 00:08:04.984
They're rebranding.

00:08:04.984 --> 00:08:07.019
"What should we do?
Should we make it gay?"

00:08:07.019 --> 00:08:08.488
Give it a rainbow ass[bleep]

00:08:08.488 --> 00:08:09.855
I don't -- just get him
out of here!

00:08:09.855 --> 00:08:13.359
That's what they were doing.

00:08:13.359 --> 00:08:15.561
The most offensive thing
about Mr. Potato Head

00:08:15.561 --> 00:08:18.097
should be that it's
still made out of plastic

00:08:18.097 --> 00:08:19.631
and it's going to be
floating in the ocean

00:08:19.631 --> 00:08:20.933
for a thousand years after

00:08:20.933 --> 00:08:22.901
you're dead
and done playing with it.

00:08:22.901 --> 00:08:24.203
That's not what it is.

00:08:24.203 --> 00:08:28.875
"Did you hear what he said about
gay plastic potatoes?

00:08:28.875 --> 00:08:31.443
Is this the America we
want to live in?"

00:08:31.443 --> 00:08:35.514
Meanwhile, there's synthetic
heroin killing people.

00:08:35.514 --> 00:08:37.483
Not important.

00:08:37.483 --> 00:08:40.352
-Hey, I have been a huge fan
of your stand-up forever,

00:08:40.352 --> 00:08:43.622
but I also feel like I was a
real early supporter of

00:08:43.622 --> 00:08:45.190
Bill Burr the actor.

00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:46.759
I think you're fantastic
in everything.

00:08:46.759 --> 00:08:49.427
You were just in
this great new show,

00:08:49.427 --> 00:08:53.132
"Reservation Dogs,"
where you're fantastic in that.

00:08:53.132 --> 00:08:56.301
And "F is for Family"
is back for another season.

00:08:56.301 --> 00:08:57.803
What is this going to be?

00:08:57.803 --> 00:08:59.137
Season 4 or 5?

00:08:59.137 --> 00:09:01.440
-This is 5.
This is the final one.

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:02.608
-Mm-hmm.

00:09:02.608 --> 00:09:05.277
-I'm not allowed to say
when it comes out

00:09:05.277 --> 00:09:10.449
or else the Netflix Algorithm
Police come after me.

00:09:10.449 --> 00:09:12.417
They know exactly
when to announce it.

00:09:12.417 --> 00:09:13.652
So I can't tell you.

00:09:13.652 --> 00:09:15.554
But it's going to be
coming out hopefully soon.

00:09:15.554 --> 00:09:20.459
And then "Reservation Dogs"
was this amazing just --

00:09:20.459 --> 00:09:22.661
I got the script for
the episode,

00:09:22.661 --> 00:09:26.164
and just beautiful writing,
the story, the whole thing,

00:09:26.164 --> 00:09:28.601
and what they
were talking about.

00:09:28.601 --> 00:09:30.302
And then I went down there,
and it was --

00:09:30.302 --> 00:09:32.405
You know, they were just
really, really cool.

00:09:32.405 --> 00:09:35.006
I had a great, great, great
time working with

00:09:35.006 --> 00:09:36.275
everybody down there,

00:09:36.275 --> 00:09:39.344
and I'm excited
that the show is doing so well.

00:09:39.344 --> 00:09:41.013
I believe it got picked up for
a second season

00:09:41.013 --> 00:09:43.582
by the time, like, the second
episode came out, which is --

00:09:43.582 --> 00:09:47.820
-And what a cool young cast,
man, of young actors.

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:48.888
-Yeah, yeah.

00:09:48.888 --> 00:09:51.823
There's -- Who'd I --
Devery Jacobs.

00:09:51.823 --> 00:09:55.828
I did most of my scenes
with her, and then --

00:09:55.828 --> 00:09:58.630
I get all the names -- Kirk Fox,
another stand-up comedian,

00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:00.432
plays the junkyard owner.

00:10:00.432 --> 00:10:04.002
Tazbah Chavez, Sterlin Harjo.

00:10:04.002 --> 00:10:05.537
I know I'm going to
forget a few other people,

00:10:05.537 --> 00:10:08.173
but, like, they just --
You know, I think Sterlin was --

00:10:08.173 --> 00:10:09.374
He came on my podcast.

00:10:09.374 --> 00:10:11.877
He's from, like, the
independent-film world,

00:10:11.877 --> 00:10:15.481
so has a really, really
strong background

00:10:15.481 --> 00:10:19.618
in just sort of doing things
that you want to do,

00:10:19.618 --> 00:10:21.453
as opposed to things
that will make money.

00:10:21.453 --> 00:10:23.588
I just think, like,
this just felt like people

00:10:23.588 --> 00:10:26.224
were doing this because they
were really connected to it.

00:10:26.224 --> 00:10:28.393
And I've been
really lucky in my career.

00:10:28.393 --> 00:10:29.795
I don't know if you've noticed.

00:10:29.795 --> 00:10:32.798
I have paratrooped into some of
the best stuff out there.

00:10:32.798 --> 00:10:35.034
I never created it.
It wasn't my idea.

00:10:35.034 --> 00:10:36.368
I didn't write it.

00:10:36.368 --> 00:10:38.104
But I come in for like
half an episode,

00:10:38.104 --> 00:10:41.874
rub up against it, and people
think I know what I'm doing.

00:10:41.874 --> 00:10:42.974
But it's not me.

00:10:42.974 --> 00:10:44.143
It's been everybody --

00:10:44.143 --> 00:10:46.244
all the Vince Gilligan-type
people out there.

00:10:46.244 --> 00:10:48.947
-Well, you have been a perfect
fit in every one of them,

00:10:48.947 --> 00:10:50.949
and it is always just
a delight to have you here.

00:10:50.949 --> 00:10:52.183
Thanks so much, Bill.

00:10:52.183 --> 00:10:53.185
-Alright, Seth.
Thank you, buddy.

00:10:53.185 --> 00:10:54.186
-Of course.

00:10:54.186 --> 00:10:55.421
The vinyl release of

00:10:55.421 --> 00:10:57.256
"Bill Burr Live From
Madison Square Garden"

00:10:57.256 --> 00:10:59.792
is available at amoeba.com
on September 23rd.

